{% tool-context
   title="Message Queue Designer"
   question="What makes a queue design reliable?"
   definition="A reliable queue design gives producers a durable place to hand off work, gives consumers bounded parallelism, and makes delivery, ordering, retry, and dead-letter behavior explicit."
   inputs="Shape producer routing, partitions, hot-key pressure, replication, consumer capacity, prefetch, acknowledgments, retries, idempotency, and ordered redrive."
   outcome="Trace backlog, oldest-message age, throughput, in-flight work, duplicate or loss exposure, dead-letter flow, and user-visible delay through one causal topology."
   experiment="Inject consumer slowdown, a poison message, broker loss, a retry storm, partition skew, and recovery. Change one contract at a time and observe which bottleneck actually moves." /%}

